Perform editorial passes in the following order, combining them locally only when doing so improves reliability:
A. Seam and continuity pass. Remove purely conversational wrappers from the reading edition, resolve redundant serial recaps, carry essential facts from old chapter headings into narrative sentences, and join scenes without unnecessary reintroductions. A recap may co2ntain a new i<project_brief>
institutional formula, or a character's repeated argument from an accidental duplicate. Ensure entrances, exits, chronology, point of view, geography, knowledge available to characters, and the consequences of their decisions remain coherent.
B. Literary line edit. Improve clarity, syntax, diction, and rhythm only where beneficial. Keep the original narrative register, dialogue meanings, pacing, political intelligence, humor, tension, and deliberate strangeness. Preserve the book's slow passages. Do not flatten speeches into summaries, make all characters equally articulate, or replace historically specific arguments with generic emotional language. Let a silence remain a silence. Let a complicated legal or diplomatic exchange remain complicated when it serves the scene. Preserve subtext and dramatic irony rather than explaining what the reader can infer.
C. Copyedit. Correct clear errors of grammar, spelling, typography, dialogue punctuation, and inconsistent styling. Respect purposeful dialect, imperfect speech, foreign-language expressions, and individual verbal habits. Do not mechanically regularize everything. Standardize names and titles only according to the established style sheet and canon. If a spelling change concerns a proper name or an in-world document, check that it does not change meaning or identity.
D. Final local review. Read the edited scene as a reader would, then compare it against its raw counterpart. Confirm that every unique event, decision, speaker, fact, promise, emotional development, in-world document, and narrative beat remains present. Check who knows what, when they know it, what they do as a result, and what their words imply. Ensure the revised text does not accidentally introduce a new allegiance, prediction, motive, historical claim, or consequential action. Record consequential changes. Routine punctuation changes may be grouped by category.
The task is revision, not replacement. Prefer minimal effective changes. Do not optimize for a numerical edit-distance target, a similarity score, or an AI detector. Use diff statistics to flag unusually invasive changes for a closer review, not to punish legitimate improvements. If a section appears substantially rewritten, compare it carefully against the original and revert unnecessary alterations. Preserve River's own narrative prose more conservatively than routine AI prose. If a genuinely significant creative improvement would require a new scene, new character, changed outcome, changed political arrangement, or altered characterization, propose it separately in the editorial notes rather than implementing it.
