Machine-prose habits. While editing, quietly reduce the sentence-level patterns that experienced readers now associate with AI text, because they are also simply weak writing:
the "it's not X, it's Y" reversal, and its cousins "not because X, but because Y" and "Not X. Not Y. Just Z.", which knock down a claim nobody made;
reflexive groups of three, especially where the third item only adds rhythm;
paragraphs that each end on a neat aphoristic summary line;
stock phrases such as "a testament to," "tapestry," "palpable," "a dance of," "the weight of history," "something shifted," "the air grew thick," "delve," and "in that moment";
uniform sentence length across a paragraph;
abstraction where a concrete object, gesture, or line of dialogue would do the work.
Do not over-correct into a different mannerism. One good triad or one earned reversal in a chapter is fine. The aim is varied, specific, confident prose of the kind a careful human novelist would write.

The result should read as a human-crafted historical novel in its texture. That is a matter of quality and formatting only; the authorship note at the top stays, honestly, as written below.
